=== layout: section

# Reading a Gene Tree

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Understanding Branch Points*

@body

- [*Branches*]{color=#355269 underline=true} [reflect evolutionary paths from common ancestors.]{color=#355269}
- [Each divergence event is marked by a]{color=#355269} [*branch point*]{color=#355269 underline=true} [in the tree.]{color=#355269}
- [These points signify the splitting of one group into two distinct lines.]{color=#355269}
- [Interpretation of these points helps trace species' evolutionary history.]{color=#355269}

![Slide image 14](assets/lect12b-gene_trees/image_014.png)

- [https\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phylogenetic\_tree](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phylogenetic_tree)

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Tracing Common Ancestors*

@body

![Slide image 15](assets/lect12b-gene_trees/image_015.png)

- [Closeness of species determined by recency of common ancestors.]{color=#55215B}
- [To find the most recent common ancestor (MRCA), &#8220;walk backwards&#8221; to where species lines converge.]{color=#55215B}
- [Example\: A and B share a more recent ancestor than B and C, indicating closer relation.]{color=#55215B}
- [Understanding MRCA is crucial for interpreting evolutionary relationships.]{color=#55215B}

- [https\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Most\_recent\_common\_ancestor](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Most_recent_common_ancestor)

=== layout: one-panel

# *Different Forms, Same Gene Tree*

@body

![Slide image 16](assets/lect12b-gene_trees/image_016.png)

- The three trees above represent identical relationships among species A, B, C, D, and E.
- The same evolutionary relationships can be represented in multiple tree forms.
- Orientation of branches doesn&#8217;t change the underlying genetic connections.

- [https\://www.khanacademy.org/science/biology/her/tree-of-life/a/phylogenetic-trees](https://www.khanacademy.org/science/biology/her/tree-of-life/a/phylogenetic-trees)

=== layout: one-panel

# *Rotating Branches in Trees*

@body

![Slide image 17](assets/lect12b-gene_trees/image_017.png)

=== layout: two-over-one-panels

# *Trees, trees, and trees, oh my*

@top-left

![Slide image 17](assets/lect12b-gene_trees/image_017.png)

@top-right

- [Another critical point is that if you rotate the structures, about the branch points as a pivot, you don&#8217;t change the relationships.]{color=#8D281E}
- [So just like the two trees left, which show the same relationships, all of the trees below show the same relationships among four species.]{color=#8D281E}
- [Despite different appearances, the underlying relationships remain constant.]{color=#8D281E}

@bottom

![Slide image 18](assets/lect12b-gene_trees/image_018.png)

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Polytomy in Gene Trees*

@body

![Slide image 19](assets/lect12b-gene_trees/image_019.png)

- [*Polytomy*]{color=#784B04 underline=true}[\: A branch point with three or more diverging species.]{color=#784B04}
- [Indicates lack of sufficient information to determine precise branching order.]{color=#784B04}
- [Common in complex evolutionary scenarios.]{color=#784B04}

- [https\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polytomy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polytomy)

